.TH BUDYK 8 "2026-10-17" "budyk 0.7.0"
.SH NAME
budyk \- lightweight server monitoring with adaptive collection
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B budyk
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B serve
Start the monitoring daemon and web server.
.TP
.B tui
Start the terminal UI.
.TP
.B hash\-password
Generate an Argon2id password hash for web authentication.
.TP
.B suggest\-rules
Generate monitoring rule suggestions from collected history.
.TP
\fBfsck\fR [\fB\-\-repair\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fIdata_dir\fR]
Check every ring file in the data directory (the one in the config if
none is given): the header, then each record (CRC, level, time order).
Damaged records are reported (readers skip them; they cannot be
repaired), and so are records of another level and a clock that stepped
back.
.B \-\-repair
counts in the records written past the index and never counted, which
a crash between a record's write and the index update leaves; it needs
the daemon stopped. A check without it can run while the daemon does.
Exit status: 0 clean, 1 problems found and repaired, 4 problems left,
8 the check could not run (usage, config, data directory, lock held).
.TP
.B version
Print version and exit.
.SH DESCRIPTION
budyk is a self-contained server monitoring daemon. It collects system
metrics using an adaptive 3-level model (heartbeat / watchful / active),
stores them in tiered ring-buffer files, and serves them via a real-time
web dashboard and terminal TUI.
.PP
Each level has its own ring file in the data directory:
.I tier1.ring
(L3),
.I tier2.ring
(L2),
.I tier3.ring
(L1) and
.IR level- name .ring
for each custom level. While L3 or a custom level collects, its samples
are also folded into one record per L2 interval in
.I tier2.ring
and one per L1 interval in
.IR tier3.ring :
means for CPU, memory, swap, load, disk and network, maxima for process
counts, temperature and RSS, the minimum for entropy. The coarse rings
keep their cadence and keep a summary after the raw samples have been
overwritten. The history API marks such records
.B folded
and, for the whole-history view, shows each stretch once, at the finest
level still on disk.
.PP
Each level reads its own set of metrics,
\fBcollection.\fIlevel\fB.metrics\fR:
.B minimal
(cpu, mem, swap, load, uptime),
.B standard
(every group),
.B full
(also each core, disk, interface and filesystem) or a list of names;
standard at L1 and full elsewhere by default. A rule that reads a group
its level does not collect is skipped for that tick.
.B collection.mode
is
.B adaptive
(the default),
.B always
(L3 all the time) or
.B on-demand
(collect only while a client is connected, and write nothing to the
rings).
.PP
A record that reached its slot before a crash but not the index is
counted in when the daemon starts again (and by
.BR "budyk fsck \-\-repair" ),
so a crash of the daemon loses no stored record.
.PP
Monitoring rules are written in Lua and evaluated on each collection tick.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/etc/budyk/config.yaml
Main configuration file.
.TP
.I /usr/local/etc/budyk/rules.lua
Lua monitoring rules.
.TP
.I /var/db/budyk/
Ring-buffer data directory.
.TP
.I /var/db/budyk/budyk.lock
Held (flock) by the running daemon, with its pid inside, so a second
daemon on the same directory, and
.BR "budyk fsck \-\-repair" ,
refuse to start. The kernel releases it however the daemon exits.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGTERM, SIGINT
Graceful shutdown — flushes rule state, closes the HTTP server, writes
the fold windows still open and closes the ring files.
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Reload the rules file specified by
.I rules.path
in the config without dropping the daemon. Per-rule
.IR cooldown_remaining ,
.I consecutive_hits
and
.I fire_count
are preserved across the swap by name, so rules in mid-cooldown stay
quiet. Rules removed from the new file are dropped; rules added pick
up zero state. The HTTP server, scheduler, alert channels, and
storage rings are untouched. Config-level changes (listen, data_dir,
collection cadence) still require a full restart.
